WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.125 --> 00:00:05.252
Hello.

00:00:05.961 --> 00:00:07.045
So as you know,

00:00:07.045 --> 00:00:09.172
my comfort zone isn’t here.

00:00:09.381 --> 00:00:10.715
It’s usually on set,

00:00:10.715 --> 00:00:12.384
behind a camera,

00:00:12.384 --> 00:00:13.426
like him,

00:00:13.426 --> 00:00:14.427
or him.

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:16.221
But I’m very happy to be here.

00:00:16.805 --> 00:00:19.975
So yes, I was born in Beirut, Lebanon.

00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:22.561
It’s what I call my home country.

00:00:23.061 --> 00:00:25.772
It’s the place
where my first memories are,

00:00:25.772 --> 00:00:27.899
where my parents live,

00:00:27.899 --> 00:00:29.734
where my first loves are,

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:31.111
my first heartbreaks.

00:00:31.528 --> 00:00:33.238
I’ve lived in other places

00:00:33.238 --> 00:00:35.156
and I’ve made them home,

00:00:35.156 --> 00:00:36.157
like New York,

00:00:36.157 --> 00:00:37.909
which I’ve fallen in love with --

00:00:37.909 --> 00:00:38.994
and in.

00:00:39.077 --> 00:00:42.956
But I always felt
like my biggest strength

00:00:42.956 --> 00:00:46.668
came from the fact that I knew
exactly where I came from.

00:00:47.127 --> 00:00:50.005
And that knowledge
was very important to me

00:00:50.005 --> 00:00:53.049
because it really defines
who I am as a woman.

00:00:54.092 --> 00:00:56.928
But growing up in Lebanon
comes with a price.

00:00:56.928 --> 00:01:00.390
I think this tension and this --

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.477
what I have between my home country 
is something I cherish

00:01:04.477 --> 00:01:06.271
but it’s also a burden,

00:01:06.271 --> 00:01:09.733
because Lebanon is a place
with a very contradicting soul.

00:01:09.858 --> 00:01:13.445
It’s a place filled with chaos and poetry;

00:01:13.445 --> 00:01:18.533
a place where hope and despair coexist
in really strange ways.

00:01:18.908 --> 00:01:23.913
It’s also a place where joy
and sorrow are inseparable,

00:01:23.913 --> 00:01:25.498
like Khalil Gibran --

00:01:25.498 --> 00:01:28.168
in one of my favorite poems
by our national poet,

00:01:28.168 --> 00:01:32.589
Khalil Gibran mentions “that well
from which comes our laughter

00:01:32.589 --> 00:01:34.716
is also the one that hosts our tears.”

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:38.053
And I think today, more than ever,
this is true in Lebanon,

00:01:38.053 --> 00:01:40.847
because after everything that happened,

00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:43.642
it feels like a land of broken dreams,

00:01:43.642 --> 00:01:46.561
but filled with so many
dreams nonetheless.

00:01:46.936 --> 00:01:49.314
And growing up in Lebanon,

00:01:49.314 --> 00:01:51.650
we were constantly
on the verge of the worst.

00:01:51.775 --> 00:01:55.570
We felt like that silence
between [one] crisis and the other

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:59.032
was almost more agonizing
than the crisis itself.

00:01:59.407 --> 00:02:01.576
And that really defined us
as human beings,

00:02:01.576 --> 00:02:04.954
because we really live every day
as if it were our last,

00:02:04.954 --> 00:02:08.333
and that’s in the best
and the worst kind of ways.

00:02:09.542 --> 00:02:12.921
I think this is where
the screenwriter in me was born:

00:02:12.921 --> 00:02:14.547
at home in Lebanon,

00:02:14.547 --> 00:02:16.132
in the streets at home

00:02:16.132 --> 00:02:18.009
and the house I grew up in,

00:02:18.009 --> 00:02:22.847
because I became fascinated
with human flaws and vulnerabilities

00:02:22.847 --> 00:02:26.643
and the truth that comes out of us
in times of crisis

00:02:26.643 --> 00:02:28.269
and when we’re put under pressure.

00:02:28.728 --> 00:02:30.355
And when at home,

00:02:30.355 --> 00:02:32.273
when I saw the people I loved the most,

00:02:32.273 --> 00:02:33.400
my parents,

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.401
be real,

00:02:34.401 --> 00:02:36.236
I felt free somehow.

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:38.947
It wasn’t always pretty 
but at least it felt safe,

00:02:38.947 --> 00:02:41.950
like this is a place
where we can be ourselves.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.955
But in 2020,

00:02:46.955 --> 00:02:50.750
when the pandemic hit the planet,

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.794
we all started questioning
what home meant.

00:02:53.211 --> 00:02:54.796
My parents were architects --

00:02:54.796 --> 00:02:56.131
are architects,

00:02:56.131 --> 00:03:01.094
so they also added to what I felt home
was to my definition of home.

00:03:01.594 --> 00:03:04.931
Because before following
my own dreams --

00:03:04.931 --> 00:03:06.808
being a filmmaker --

00:03:06.808 --> 00:03:07.892
I was a good daughter,

00:03:07.892 --> 00:03:08.893
a good girl,

00:03:08.893 --> 00:03:10.478
and I followed my father’s dreams

00:03:10.478 --> 00:03:12.397
and I studied architecture and finished.

00:03:12.814 --> 00:03:19.571
And what I learned in architecture school
is how much you can learn about people,

00:03:19.571 --> 00:03:20.572
about their story,

00:03:20.572 --> 00:03:24.367
about societies through the spaces
that they inhabit,

00:03:24.367 --> 00:03:25.410
through every object,

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.287
every frame, every wall,

00:03:27.287 --> 00:03:28.288
through the ground,

00:03:28.288 --> 00:03:29.289
through the streets.

00:03:29.622 --> 00:03:30.623
But what do you do

00:03:30.623 --> 00:03:34.043
when you feel like the ground
on which you’re standing might not hold?

00:03:34.461 --> 00:03:35.754
In the world of today,

00:03:35.754 --> 00:03:38.298
filled with political instability,

00:03:38.298 --> 00:03:42.802
climate disasters, where our spaces
are constantly ravaged and threatened,

00:03:42.802 --> 00:03:44.679
how do you create a sense of home?

00:03:45.013 --> 00:03:47.766
In 2020 when the pandemic hit,

00:03:47.766 --> 00:03:48.767
we all felt --

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:51.478
or at least those of us
lucky enough to have homes --

00:03:51.478 --> 00:03:52.562
we all went inside,

00:03:52.562 --> 00:03:54.314
and that became our safe space.

00:03:54.689 --> 00:03:57.192
The outside world became the threat:

00:03:57.192 --> 00:03:58.359
the air, the people.

00:03:58.359 --> 00:04:00.361
This invisible monster was outside.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.072
But as long as you were
tucked in your bubble,

00:04:03.072 --> 00:04:04.073
you were safe.

00:04:04.407 --> 00:04:05.992
And I’m talking about those of us

00:04:05.992 --> 00:04:09.579
who are lucky enough not to live
locked with an abuser,

00:04:09.579 --> 00:04:11.247
victims of domestic abuse.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.124
So for those of us,

00:04:13.124 --> 00:04:14.751
the safe bubble was inside.

00:04:15.376 --> 00:04:16.544
Or so we thought.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:21.341
On August 4, 2020 in Lebanon,

00:04:21.341 --> 00:04:22.509
our lives changed.

00:04:23.176 --> 00:04:24.511
In a split of a second,

00:04:24.511 --> 00:04:28.389
one of the largest non-nuclear
explosions pulverized our port

00:04:28.389 --> 00:04:30.600
and destroyed half our city,

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:31.601
killing many people

00:04:31.601 --> 00:04:32.769
and destroying homes

00:04:32.769 --> 00:04:35.897
and creating losses
that we can’t even count until today.

00:04:36.231 --> 00:04:39.651
And there still hasn’t been
accountability for what happened,

00:04:39.651 --> 00:04:44.072
even though it was the result of years
of political mismanagement and corruption.

00:04:44.781 --> 00:04:48.159
I happened, on that day, to be in Beirut:

00:04:48.159 --> 00:04:49.327
in the center of Beirut,

00:04:49.327 --> 00:04:50.411
in the office,

00:04:50.411 --> 00:04:53.873
because I was in pre-production
for my first feature film,

00:04:53.873 --> 00:04:55.542
“Costa Brava, Lebanon,”

00:04:55.542 --> 00:05:00.046
a film we had been working on
for a few years really hard,

00:05:00.046 --> 00:05:05.718
and a film that, ironically, is the story
of a family that decides to leave Beirut,

00:05:05.718 --> 00:05:08.471
a place that doesn’t feel safe
to them anymore

00:05:08.471 --> 00:05:11.349
to create a utopic mountain home,

00:05:11.349 --> 00:05:13.101
a self-sustainable mountain home

00:05:13.101 --> 00:05:15.520
away from a city
that has broken their hearts.

00:05:16.187 --> 00:05:17.689
This is the cast of the film.

00:05:17.981 --> 00:05:19.148
And then what happens

00:05:19.148 --> 00:05:22.443
is that their utopia
is completely destroyed

00:05:22.443 --> 00:05:23.778
when the government decides

00:05:23.778 --> 00:05:27.740
to build an illegal garbage landfill
right outside their home,

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.285
bringing that reality
to their front door --

00:05:30.285 --> 00:05:33.037
the one they have been
running away from for many years.

00:05:33.663 --> 00:05:38.501
The family finds itself
again confronted to this destruction

00:05:38.501 --> 00:05:41.004
that it had been trying to avoid,

00:05:41.004 --> 00:05:44.048
facing everything it was trying
to protect itself from.

00:05:45.216 --> 00:05:49.095
I was with the crew, the cast
and the crew of the film

00:05:49.095 --> 00:05:51.639
in the office in Gemmayze in Beirut,

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:52.974
when at six or seven,

00:05:52.974 --> 00:05:54.559
in the split of a second,

00:05:54.559 --> 00:05:56.352
our lives were turned upside down.

00:05:56.895 --> 00:06:00.023
We went from a creative meeting
filled with passion and love

00:06:00.023 --> 00:06:01.900
and excitement

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.736
to looking for each other under rubble,

00:06:04.736 --> 00:06:06.738
wondering if we had all made it alive.

00:06:07.363 --> 00:06:09.616
Luckily, we did,

00:06:09.616 --> 00:06:13.578
and we were much luckier
than a lot of people in the same street.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:16.164
My cinematographer, Joe,
almost lost his eye

00:06:16.164 --> 00:06:17.498
and everyone was injured.

00:06:19.083 --> 00:06:21.085
We got out of the street

00:06:21.085 --> 00:06:26.174
and realized that the explosion
was not just next to the office,

00:06:26.174 --> 00:06:27.175
but everywhere,

00:06:27.175 --> 00:06:29.469
and that’s when we understood
how big it was.

00:06:30.053 --> 00:06:32.597
Walking down the street like zombies
around that time,

00:06:32.597 --> 00:06:36.893
surrounded by broken,
confused, stunned faces,

00:06:36.893 --> 00:06:41.439
felt like walking in the set of a movie
I don’t want to direct or be a part of.

00:06:42.357 --> 00:06:43.775
Everyone’s homes,

00:06:43.775 --> 00:06:44.984
their private spaces,

00:06:44.984 --> 00:06:47.153
their frames, their walls,

00:06:47.153 --> 00:06:49.072
were dust on which we were walking.

00:06:51.282 --> 00:06:53.159
We stopped everything at that moment

00:06:53.159 --> 00:06:55.203
because we lost all of our coordinates:

00:06:55.203 --> 00:06:56.996
all of our sense of home,

00:06:56.996 --> 00:06:58.748
everything that we had worked for.

00:06:59.374 --> 00:07:03.711
So what we did is we just took
a moment for two months

00:07:03.711 --> 00:07:05.797
and each of us took time to grieve,

00:07:05.797 --> 00:07:07.090
to assess the losses,

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:09.175
whether it was the office or all of us.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:13.012
Anyway, how can you even think
about being creative

00:07:13.012 --> 00:07:14.931
or making anything

00:07:14.931 --> 00:07:17.600
at a moment where you feel
like you’re living hell --

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.394
in the middle of hell?

00:07:19.894 --> 00:07:21.896
You cannot create amidst such chaos.

00:07:22.355 --> 00:07:23.356
At that moment,

00:07:23.356 --> 00:07:24.357
my mother --

00:07:24.357 --> 00:07:25.400
my hero on that day

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:29.278
because it’s only thanks to her
that some of us made it to a hospital,

00:07:29.278 --> 00:07:31.155
who has lived civil wars --

00:07:31.155 --> 00:07:34.033
reminded me of a book I read
in architecture school.

00:07:34.784 --> 00:07:37.161
“Invisible Cities” by Italo Calvino.

00:07:37.745 --> 00:07:41.249
I’ll read to you the quote
that she read to me at that time

00:07:41.249 --> 00:07:43.918
when she saw the despair
me and my team were in.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:49.298
“The hell of the living
is not something that will be.

00:07:49.966 --> 00:07:51.092
If there is one,

00:07:51.092 --> 00:07:52.719
it is what is already here,

00:07:52.719 --> 00:07:56.180
the hell we live every day,
that we make by being together.

00:07:56.806 --> 00:07:59.100
There are two ways to escape suffering it.

00:07:59.517 --> 00:08:01.144
The first is easy for many:

00:08:01.519 --> 00:08:02.520
accept the hell,

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.440
and become such a part of it
that you can no longer see it.

00:08:06.065 --> 00:08:07.066
The second is risky

00:08:07.066 --> 00:08:09.819
and demands constant vigilance
and apprehension:

00:08:10.278 --> 00:08:15.658
seek and learn to recognize who and what,
in the midst of hell, are not hell,

00:08:15.658 --> 00:08:18.161
then make them endure,
give them space.”

00:08:18.745 --> 00:08:21.039
Luckily, these people
were not too far from me.

00:08:21.122 --> 00:08:23.666
There were the cast
and the crew of this film,

00:08:23.666 --> 00:08:27.128
so we met all together and brainstormed.

00:08:27.211 --> 00:08:29.505
We thought, should we make
this film or not?

00:08:29.964 --> 00:08:33.301
It seemed crazy to make anything
around that time in Lebanon

00:08:33.301 --> 00:08:36.304
because the country
was experiencing, until now,

00:08:36.304 --> 00:08:39.724
its worst economic crisis
since its inception,

00:08:39.724 --> 00:08:41.100
the loss and the destruction

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:44.729
and the PTSD we were
all going through after the explosion

00:08:44.729 --> 00:08:46.647
and also the global pandemic,

00:08:46.647 --> 00:08:49.275
which was hitting the country really hard,

00:08:49.275 --> 00:08:50.985
which we had almost forgotten about

00:08:50.985 --> 00:08:53.154
because of everything else
that was happening.

00:08:53.446 --> 00:08:57.408
But at a moment where existing
felt like an act of resistance,

00:08:57.408 --> 00:09:00.995
we felt like making this movie
was very important,

00:09:00.995 --> 00:09:03.081
because it would mean regaining agency --

00:09:03.081 --> 00:09:04.624
to regain agency

00:09:04.624 --> 00:09:07.293
and feel like they haven't
taken everything from us.

00:09:08.294 --> 00:09:09.837
And as Maya Angelou says,

00:09:09.837 --> 00:09:11.547
there’s nothing more agonizing

00:09:11.547 --> 00:09:14.133
than an untold story
hanging inside of you --

00:09:14.133 --> 00:09:15.718
not directly quoting.

00:09:16.302 --> 00:09:19.972
And I think we really needed
to regain a sense of order,

00:09:20.264 --> 00:09:21.474
find our coordinate,

00:09:21.474 --> 00:09:22.642
a sense of home.

00:09:22.934 --> 00:09:24.560
And like after World War I,

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:27.980
a lot of European artists
went back into classicism,

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:31.651
trying to run away
from this feeling of destruction

00:09:31.651 --> 00:09:32.944
that the war had brought in

00:09:32.944 --> 00:09:36.864
and stepped away from
the experimentalism that came before,

00:09:36.864 --> 00:09:40.701
I think we used creativity
to rebuild those pillars and that order.

00:09:41.744 --> 00:09:43.079
So it was a crazy decision,

00:09:43.079 --> 00:09:45.164
but we did it because we wanted to

00:09:45.164 --> 00:09:47.250
and because something was driving us.

00:09:47.583 --> 00:09:50.962
So we went and made the film
against all odds.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.297
And it was hard,

00:09:53.297 --> 00:09:55.091
it was filled with obstacles,

00:09:55.091 --> 00:09:56.134
but it was beautiful,

00:09:56.134 --> 00:09:59.887
because at a moment
where we had missed human connection

00:09:59.887 --> 00:10:05.184
and at a moment where our societies
are becoming more fragile and loveless,

00:10:05.184 --> 00:10:08.104
we were able to recreate
a moment of warmth,

00:10:08.104 --> 00:10:09.605
of love and magic,

00:10:09.605 --> 00:10:12.275
at a moment where it was hard to find any.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:14.443
And I think that that was very special

00:10:14.443 --> 00:10:18.656
because telling the story
together gave us ...

00:10:18.656 --> 00:10:19.949
a sense of home again.

00:10:19.949 --> 00:10:22.285
It felt like the set became
that safe space,

00:10:22.285 --> 00:10:23.286
that family.

00:10:23.286 --> 00:10:27.999
And it was as real and as raw
as the home I was telling you about.

00:10:27.999 --> 00:10:30.668
Because we were all filled with creativity

00:10:30.668 --> 00:10:32.545
and a desire to make something,

00:10:32.545 --> 00:10:35.006
but we are also grieving and broken.

00:10:35.173 --> 00:10:37.466
So that was me again,

00:10:37.466 --> 00:10:41.846
realizing the beauty of being surrounded
by people as real as me,

00:10:41.846 --> 00:10:44.724
even if it was not always pretty,

00:10:44.724 --> 00:10:45.725
but it was real.

00:10:46.142 --> 00:10:49.729
And I think that courage and --

00:10:49.729 --> 00:10:54.567
we were always told to go to that place
that is the place of great pain

00:10:54.567 --> 00:10:57.528
because it’s also a place
of great inspiration.

00:10:58.154 --> 00:11:00.948
I think that it’s easy to hear and to say,

00:11:00.948 --> 00:11:02.700
but it’s really hard to achieve.

00:11:02.867 --> 00:11:04.577
I think that courage to go there,

00:11:04.577 --> 00:11:07.079
to go where it hurts
when you’re so broken,

00:11:07.079 --> 00:11:08.664
came to me from those people,

00:11:08.664 --> 00:11:10.208
this cast and crew

00:11:10.208 --> 00:11:14.545
that really, really gave me the courage

00:11:14.545 --> 00:11:15.755
to want to tell the story

00:11:15.755 --> 00:11:17.965
and reminded me of the importance of it.

00:11:18.466 --> 00:11:21.594
And I want to mention
also those two girls.

00:11:21.636 --> 00:11:24.764
I actually have twins
who played the role of Rim,

00:11:24.764 --> 00:11:26.515
the protagonist of the film,

00:11:26.515 --> 00:11:27.975
and they both shared the role.

00:11:28.226 --> 00:11:31.896
And I think working with them
was a great learning experience for me,

00:11:31.896 --> 00:11:35.191
because that’s the beauty
of being a filmmaker.

00:11:35.191 --> 00:11:37.151
You work with people from different ages

00:11:37.151 --> 00:11:39.028
and different backgrounds all the time,

00:11:39.028 --> 00:11:40.196
and working with them,

00:11:40.196 --> 00:11:41.364
for all of us on set,

00:11:41.364 --> 00:11:45.701
was a reminder of the importance
of remaining hopeful

00:11:45.701 --> 00:11:47.662
and keeping the sense of wonder,

00:11:47.662 --> 00:11:49.413
especially for their generation.

00:11:49.413 --> 00:11:52.083
Because whatever world
we’re fighting for today,

00:11:52.083 --> 00:11:54.210
they will be able to benefit from.

00:11:54.919 --> 00:11:59.257
And so I know that we all deal with loss
and rebuilding a home that we lost

00:11:59.257 --> 00:12:00.424
in different ways.

00:12:00.633 --> 00:12:02.927
For me, it was through human connection

00:12:02.927 --> 00:12:05.930
and understanding that it wasn't
necessarily a space anymore.

00:12:06.347 --> 00:12:08.599
And I think for you
it might be something else.

00:12:08.599 --> 00:12:10.768
We all channel that in different ways.

00:12:11.185 --> 00:12:12.186
But for me,

00:12:12.186 --> 00:12:16.524
that moment of joy, of sorrow, of freedom,

00:12:16.524 --> 00:12:17.817
of creativity,

00:12:17.817 --> 00:12:20.486
that moment between
the “action” and the “cut,”

00:12:20.486 --> 00:12:22.154
that’s what felt like home.

00:12:22.613 --> 00:12:24.115
And I’m very grateful for that.

